WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.100
These days, it seems like everyone has Netflix.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.180
But is everyone fully utilizing the service?

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.460
Are they getting their money's worth, devouring
the films and shows they like in the most

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.120
efficient manner?

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:13.220
Here are some Netflix tricks, hacks, and add-ons
to enhance the streaming experience.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.180
While a lot of people watch Netflix on a smart
TV, countless more binge-watch shows and movies

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.100
the old-fashioned way: on a laptop or desktop
computer.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.390
Netflix's instant viewing service was available
on computers long before smart TVs came along,

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.140
and there are some basic functions of watching
that way that are still embedded in the Netflix

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:30.680
API.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.440
Like most any other computer program, Netflix's
streaming code responds to "hot keys," or

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:39.899
"keyboard shortcuts," meaning certain functions
are performed by pressing specific keys - no

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.400
use of the mouse with its pointing, clicking,
and locating menus necessary.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.980
They're also intuitive, meaning the keys do
exactly what you expect.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.660
Hitting the space bar pauses, and then resumes,
a video; striking "F" allows Netflix to take

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:56.149
up the device's entire screen; "escape" shrinks
the video back down; and tapping the left

00:00:56.149 --> 00:01:00.020
and right arrows will rewind and fast-forward,
respectively, by ten seconds.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.880
Audio can also be toggled, too: the "M" mutes,
while the up and down arrows adjust the volume

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:05.380
accordingly.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.200
Even a casual scroll through Netflix will
reveal a few examples of the service's intricate

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.620
categorization system.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.860
Thanks to complicated algorithms that factor
in an individual's viewing history, it will

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.280
recommend exactly the kinds of content it
thinks a user will want to watch, such as

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.560
"Thrillers Featuring a Strong Female Lead"
or "Goofy Showbiz Movies."

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.960
Netflix has thousands of these classifications
and they've all got an assigned code number

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.560
for the sake of clean data storage.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.500
These codes are directly accessible when using
Netflix on a web browser.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:39.940
Simply type "www.netflix.com/browse/genre/"
into your browser's navigation bar, and after

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:44.080
that last slash, type in a three-, four- or
five-digit number at random and see what you

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:44.580
get.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.700
Or, try out a few.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.500
The categorizations can be broken down into
broad genres; for example, documentaries can

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.020
be found at 6839, horror at 8711.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:59.040
The categorizations can also be incredibly
specific: sports movies, but only ones about

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.100
martial arts, boxing, and wrestling are at
6695.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.700
There are plenty of circumstances in which
streaming things on Netflix isn't advisable

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:07.520
or attractive.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.860
If only a person could download a show or
movie to their device, and then watch it whenever

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.200
they wanted, with no internet connection required.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:19.100
Fortunately, this is a very convenient feature
that Netflix quietly added to its streaming

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.760
plans way back in 2016.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:25.320
If the content producer has opted in on downloadability,
there's a button right below the movie or

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:26.540
episode's description.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.320
Simply click it, wait a few minutes, and it's
ready to go.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.820
Watching a movie before Netflix was something
of an ordeal.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:36.960
Every night, millions would descend on their
local Blockbuster or another retail rental

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.100
chain and make a beeline for the new releases
wall only to find that they were all gone.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.880
Netflix eliminated this disappointment once
and for all by streaming a wide catalog of

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.720
movies and TV shows, adding more every few
days.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.770
The only problem with this is that Netflix
doesn't clearly delineate what's actually

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:53.260
new.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.120
They've got a "trending" section, but otherwise
it's going to take a third-party client to

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.780
figure out what is actually, truly new on
Netflix.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.560
That's the whole point of Flixable.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.459
It's a recommendation app that sorts through
Netflix, and can be customized according to

00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:09.959
genre, user ratings, language, release year,
and most attractively, the date it was added

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.160
to the streaming service.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.180
Its default setting lists the newest additions
at the top, and it works backwards as you

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.280
scroll down the page.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.520
Allow Flixable to access your Netflix account,
and you can play movies from the service with

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:21.520
a couple of clicks.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.420
With an interconnected world that loves to
binge entire series as soon as they hit Netflix,

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.692
it's hard to keep spoilers at bay for someone
who wants to stretch out the viewing of a

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:30.340
series.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:35.520
Avoiding leaks of plot twists or shocking
character developments is tough, but not impossible.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.620
But sometimes Netflix will go and spoil everything
anyway.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.020
It's not like the service means to, but those
brief plot descriptions displayed for every

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:47.460
episode that pop up on screen when a series
is selected can reveal a lot of details.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:53.000
Enter Super Netflix, an add-on Chrome extension
that makes for a truly spoiler-free experience.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:57.230
It blurs plot descriptions of individual episodes
while also obscuring the thumbnail images

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.520
accompanying those synopses.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.700
Like virtually every other digital media platform,
Netflix offers users a feature whereby they

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.300
can make a list of movies and TV series they
can bookmark for future viewing.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.840
"My List" is necessary for Netflix, since
it's easy to get overwhelmed with choices.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:16.100
That's why, by clicking a few buttons, Netflix
users can populate their "List."

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:20.380
The only problem is that the feature has one
major flaw in that it can't be rearranged.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.500
Unlike the old, toggle-able Netflix DVD-by-mail
queue, titles are listed by the order in which

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.100
they're added.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.060
However, buried deep in Netflix's user settings
is a way to manually rearrange the "My List"

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:31.560
list.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.559
It looks just like that Netflix DVD queue,
except that it's got all those things you

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:38.000
can watch right now… or later, as the case
may be.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.520
From one's account page, look for "Order in
My Lists," and then toggle "Manual Ordering."

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.260
Watching a movie on the couch with another
person is a true American pastime, dating

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:50.000
back all the way to the '80s with the explosion
of the VCR.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:54.160
Spending the evening watching a movie or a
few episodes of a show is pretty much the

00:04:54.169 --> 00:04:58.939
same now, only the content is delivered via
high-speed internet, and because of that technological

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:02.020
breakthrough, you no longer have to be in
the same room with somebody to watch a movie

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:02.520
together.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:07.139
Thanks to a PC and Apple application called
Kast, friends at different colleges, a parent

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:11.780
and kid separated by a business trip, or people
urged to stay home during a pandemic can share

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:12.420
entertainment.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:15.800
"Watching tv with only one person just isn't
natural."

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.020
One part video chat service, one part screen-sharing
software, Kast has an easily navigable interface

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:25.340
that allows both small groups or collectives
of more than 100 people to all gather around

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:29.330
their monitors, and watch the same video as
if you were all sitting on the same couch.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.600
And, of course, everyone can text-chat about
the video in real-time.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:37.860
Score, dialogue, and sound effects are all
crucial elements of the cinematic experience,

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.900
but film and TV are still visual media.

00:05:40.229 --> 00:05:44.480
Nevertheless, the picture-perfect cinematography,
breathtaking landscapes, and physical acting

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:49.479
subtleties should still be enjoyed by anyone
and everyone, even those who lack sight entirely

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:51.180
or are otherwise impaired visually.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:54.600
Luckily, Netflix offers a free service called
"Audio Descriptions."

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:58.480
According to the streaming service, it's an
"optional narration that describes what is

00:05:58.480 --> 00:05:59.820
happening on-screen."

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:04.540
The dulcet tones of a neutral narrator dutifully
describe everything that's happening on screen,

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:08.040
such as physical movements, costumes, settings,
and scene transitions.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:14.680
"The brunette lifeguard smiles flirtatiously
as she walks past Billy, who pays no mind

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:17.660
to her or any of the ogling females in his
wake."

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:18.840
Isn't that fun?

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:23.300
Audio Description is currently most available
on Netflix's deep canon of original productions,

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:27.080
and is sometimes available on movies and TV
shows produced out-of-house.

00:06:27.589 --> 00:06:31.820
It's a great way to make movies more accessible
to those who can't see every detail, and allows

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:35.200
those with full vision to enjoy a favorite
film or series in a whole new way.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:39.879
Netflix got its start in the United States,
and as English is the most commonly spoken

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:42.820
tongue there, most of the service's content
is in that language.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:47.560
That includes blockbusters, indie movies,
licensed TV shows, and original productions.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:52.460
That said, Netflix also offers an increasingly
abundant array of movies and TV shows from

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:55.640
around the world, produced in the languages
of their countries of origin.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:07:00.540
Whether it's an artsy French film or a reality
show from Japan, Netflix offers subtitles

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:03.040
for its viewers who speak and read only English.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.500
They'll appear in a standard, bland font in
an easy-to-read color.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:11.580
However, those might not work for everybody,
and since Netflix is all about near-infinite

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:14.140
choice, users can personally modify their
subtitles.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.620
Simply go to settings, then general, then
accessibility, and enable the "Closed Captions

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:19.720
+ SDH."

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:23.900
After selecting "style," viewers can change
up the words' font, color, and size.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:29.760
Netflix is obviously a marvel of modern engineering
and a wish come true: plenty of recorded entertainment

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.710
at one's fingertips, just a click away, and
all for a monthly fee of less than the cost

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:34.320
of a pizza.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:37.020
And yet, there is still fault to be found
with the service.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:41.260
There are certain mechanics of watching Netflix,
features that take one out of the immersive

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.500
viewing experience, that are just plain annoying.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:48.139
Some people don't like how, if they stop scrolling
to read a synopsis, a blaring preview of that

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:50.020
selection automatically plays.

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:54.880
Not everyone binge-watching a show wants to
see the title sequence play every darn time.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:58.580
And let's not forget how the service periodically
asks, "Are you still watching?"

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:02.820
The developers of a Chrome extension called
Never Ending Netflix put a critical eye on

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.600
the streamer to offer up a workaround of all
those annoyances.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:10.279
With a series of check boxes to toggle, Never
Ending Netflix can allow users to always skip

00:08:10.279 --> 00:08:14.869
a title sequence, auto play the next episode,
turn off credits, kill auto-play previews,

00:08:14.869 --> 00:08:16.369
and eliminate the "still watching?"

00:08:16.369 --> 00:08:18.120
prompt, among other opt-in selections.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.640
Even the most wonderful new technologies can
have a downside.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:27.099
As Netflix's movie-and-TV streaming platform
spread around a delighted world in the 2010s,

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.540
it brought with it a brand-new social phenomenon
called "Netflix cheating."

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:33.960
Let's say a couple discovers a marvelous TV
series on Netflix.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:38.640
Every episode ends in a cliffhanger, necessitating
the need to watch far too many in one evening

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.160
because it's just so gripping.

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:44.520
At some point, bedtime arrives and the couple
has to turn off the TV... but that's when

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:45.600
the cheating occurs.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.180
While the husband sleeps, for example, his
wife sneaks out of the bedroom and watches

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:52.380
a few more episodes by herself, without waiting
for her partner-in-television.

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.560
"What do you want to watch?...

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:57.900
"How about Orange is the new black?

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:00.740
Oh wait you probably want to watch that with Jeremy?  Oh no...

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:01.940
It's totally fine."

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:06.580
Netflix cheating really does speak to a betrayal
of trust, not unlike other things spouses

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:08.840
or partners could do when the other person
isn't looking.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:12.920
We're not advocating doing this of course,
but there are ways to cover one's tracks.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:17.220
Not only could a Netflix user skip ahead a
few episodes by using an alternate profile

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:21.620
on the same Netflix account, but it's also
possible to line-edit the watch history, thereby

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:23.420
destroying the evidence of show-cheating.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.740
It's possible by heading to one's "My Profile,"
then clicking "Viewing Activity."

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:31.320
Each recently watched item appears, and next
to it a large "X."

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:33.089
Once clicked, that show disappears.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:36.500
It can be very hard to decide what to watch
on Netflix.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:40.480
A person, tired and weary at the end of a
long day of work spent making hundreds of

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:43.080
decisions, may have a hard time making just
one more.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:47.850
There's also the possibility of spouses, partners,
or families, not coming to agreement about

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:48.840
a Netflix option.

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:52.940
Wouldn't it be nice if somebody else, like
a well-meaning bot, just kind of knew what

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:56.060
you'd like, and would put on that exact movie
or TV show for you?

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:56.780
"Wait...

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:59.260
"what was the first one you said?"

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:00.820
Bloodline?

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:03.140
Let's watch that."

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:07.720
That's the idea behind Netflix Roulette, a
wild recommendation engine.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:12.350
It allows users to enter in as little or as
much criteria as they want, from what genre

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:15.300
you want to watch to whether a TV show or
a movie is preferred.

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:20.240
It even factors in how critically beloved
it is - Netflix Roulette can take Rotten Tomatoes

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:23.420
or IMDb scores of a certain critical benchmark
into account.

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:28.360
After the program has gathered all the pertinent
info, the user clicks a big green "Spin" button

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.800
to uncover a hidden gem in the streaming service.

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:33.240
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.760
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
tips are coming soon.

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:40.500
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

